That explains everything — the deploy failed because there's no Dockerfile in the repo. When you uploaded, the files got crossed (that's also why render.yaml had the FROM ghcr... line earlier — the Dockerfile's contents landed in the wrong file). So we just need to create the Dockerfile fresh.

On GitHub:

In your repo, click Add file → Create new file (not Upload).
In the filename box at the top, type exactly: Dockerfile — capital D, no extension, no .txt.
Paste this into the body:
dockerfile
FROM ghcr.io/puppeteer/puppeteer:22.12.1

ENV PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD=false \
    NODE_ENV=production

USER root
WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install --omit=dev --no-audit --no-fund

COPY . .

EXPOSE 8787
CMD ["node", "server.js"]
Click Commit changes.
That drops the missing Dockerfile at the repo root where render.yaml points (./Dockerfile). Render will auto-redeploy on the commit — or hit Manual Deploy → Deploy latest commit.

Quick gut-check while you're in there: make sure render.yaml still starts with services: (the YAML we pasted), and that package.json, server.js, store.js, mailer.js, and the public/ folder are all showing in the repo file list. If any of those are missing too, tell me which and I'll get you the contents. The Dockerfile was the one that didn't make it — create it and you should be building.

now what

