That's the exact problem, and it's the same file-crossing from the upload: your package.json doesn't contain JSON — it has the .env.example config text (# ---- RRG SSC Mailer configuration ----). npm can't parse it, so the build dies. The files' contents got shuffled during the manual creation.

Fix package.json the same way:

Repo → click package.json → pencil ✏️.
Select all, delete, paste exactly this:
json
{
  "name": "rrg-ssc-mailer",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "RRG intake mailer — receives form submissions, renders a branded PDF, emails the rep, logs to disk.",
  "main": "server.js",
  "engines": { "node": ">=18" },
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node server.js"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "cors": "^2.8.5",
    "dotenv": "^16.4.5",
    "express": "^4.19.2",
    "nodemailer": "^6.9.13",
    "puppeteer": "^22.12.1"
  }
}
Commit changes.
It'll auto-redeploy. But here's the thing — this is the third scrambled file (render.yaml, then the missing Dockerfile, now package.json), which means the upload got jumbled and other files may be wrong too (server.js, store.js, mailer.js, template.js). Rather than fix them one failed build at a time, let me stop the bleeding: commit that package.json, and if the next build fails on a different file, we'll do a clean re-do the reliable way — I'll drop the correct files straight onto your PC and you push them with GitHub Desktop, no manual pasting. Commit it and tell me what the next build says.






